1. Public URL: https://assignment-part-2-xqbg.bolt.host

2. Technology Stack Used: React 19, Vite 8, react-leaflet, Supabase, react-router-dom

3. How to run locally: 
Unzip project file then navigate to cd Part2_fyk7737/project and install dependancies using npm install.
Create a .env file in the project root a template .enc is provided in the zip.
Use npm run dev to run in development mode. nmp run build for production and npm run preview to preview the production.

4. Microservice API endpoints used (local and remote): Supabase project URL, both tables (bookings, drivers), and what operations each page uses

5. Feature descriptions:
The BookingPage component replicates the Part 1 booking.html functionality within React. It collects all required passenger inputs (name, phone, address, pickup suburb, destination suburb, date and time), validates them on the client side, and submits a booking to the Supabase bookings table. The server-side BRN generation logic from Part 1 has been moved into the bookingUtils.js helper, which queries the database for the highest existing BRN and increments it. A confirmation message with the booking reference number, pickup date, and pickup time is displayed inline after a successful submission, exactly matching the Part 1 specification.

The DriversPage component provides a driver management dashboard. It reads all registered drivers from the Supabase drivers table and displays them in a card grid layout. Dispatchers can filter drivers by availability status (All / Available / On Trip) and search by driver name, driver ID, or vehicle plate number in real time. Each driver card shows the driver's ID, phone number, vehicle model, vehicle plate, and approximate GPS coordinates. A toggle button on each card allows dispatchers to flip a driver between Available and On Trip status, which immediately updates the database and reflects in the UI without a page reload. Summary statistics (total drivers, available count, on-trip count) are shown at the top of the page.

The TrackPage component provides two sub-features accessible via a tab interface. The Live Map tab renders an interactive Leaflet map centred on Auckland. All registered drivers are plotted as green markers at their stored GPS coordinates, with popups showing the driver name, vehicle, and current availability status. Unassigned upcoming bookings whose pickup suburb matches a known coordinate in the application's suburb lookup table are plotted as blue markers, with popups showing the booking reference number, customer name, and pickup details. The map data refreshes automatically every 30 seconds. The Track Booking tab allows passengers to enter a BRN and retrieve their booking status, full booking details, and assigned driver information (if applicable) in a structured card layout.

The PaymentPage component simulates a credit card payment flow. A passenger enters their BRN to look up a booking, which populates a booking summary panel showing the customer name, pickup and destination suburbs, date and time, and current status. An estimated fare is calculated based on a deterministic distance approximation between the pickup and destination suburbs (base fare NZD $5.00 plus NZD $3.50 per estimated kilometre). The payment panel renders a live card preview that updates as the user types their card number, card holder name, and expiry date. Client-side validation checks for a 16-digit card number, a populated name, a valid MM/YY expiry, and a 3 to 4 digit CVV. On submission a 1.5-second processing delay simulates a real payment gateway, after which a success confirmation is shown with the amount paid and the last four digits of the card. No real payment processing occurs.

6. Testing Instructions: 
Bookings Page
1.	Navigate to the root URL (/) or click Book in the navigation bar.
2.	The date and time fields will be pre-filled with the current date and time.
3.	Submit the form with an empty name field to verify required-field validation.
4.	Enter a phone number shorter than 10 digits or containing letters to verify phone validation.
5.	Set the pickup date or time to a past value to verify the date-in-past validation.
6.	Fill all required fields correctly and click Book Cab.
7.	A confirmation message will appear below the form showing the booking reference number, pickup time, and pickup date.
Admin Page
1.	Navigate to /admin or click Admin in the navigation bar.
2.	Leave the search field empty and click Search Bookings to display all unassigned bookings with a pickup time within 2 hours of now.
3.	Enter BRN00002 and click Search Bookings to retrieve a specific record.
4.	Enter an invalid reference number such as XYZ123 to verify format validation.
5.	Click the Assign button on a returned row to change the status to assigned and see the confirmation message.
Driver Query Page
1.	Navigate to /drivers or click Drivers in the navigation bar.
2.	Use the filter tabs to switch between All, Available, and On Trip views.
3.	Type DRV003 in the search box to filter to a single driver.
4.	Click Mark as Available or Mark as On Trip on any driver card to toggle their status.
Live Map/Tracking Page
1.	Navigate to /track or click Track in the navigation bar.
2.	The Live Map tab loads an interactive map of Auckland with driver pins and booking pickup pins.
3.	Click any green pin to see driver details in a popup.
4.	Click any blue pin to see booking details. Blue pins only appear for bookings whose pickup suburb matches a known suburb in the lookup table (e.g. Auckland CBD, Ponsonby, Newmarket, Northcote).
5.	Switch to the Track Booking tab and enter BRN00002 to retrieve booking and status details.
6.	Enter BRN00001 to see an assigned booking where driver details would be displayed.
Payment Page
1.	Navigate to /payment or click Payment in the navigation bar.
2.	Enter BRN00003 in the lookup field and click Find Booking.
3.	The booking summary will appear on the left with an estimated fare.
4.	On the right, fill in a 16-digit card number (e.g. 4111111111111111), a cardholder name, expiry (e.g. 12/27), and CVV (e.g. 123).
5.	The card preview on screen updates live as you type.
6.	Click the Pay button and wait 1.5 seconds for the simulated processing animation.
7.	A success confirmation screen will display the amount paid and masked card number.


7. Any limitations or known issues: 
•	Payment is fully simulated. No real payment gateway integration is implemented. Card numbers are not validated beyond a digit count and no Luhn check is performed. The estimated fare is calculated from a deterministic hash of the suburb names and does not reflect actual distances.

•	Driver GPS coordinates are static values stored in the database and are not updated in real time. Driver movement simulation is not implemented. Pickup suburb coordinates are resolved from a hard-coded lookup table of approximately 15 Auckland suburbs. Bookings with suburbs not in the lookup table (e.g. freeform addresses) will not appear as map pins.

•	The Admin page Assign button updates the booking status to assigned but does not automatically link a specific driver record to the booking. The driver_id field in the bookings table remains null after assignment unless manually set. This means the Track Booking page will show assigned status but no driver details for bookings assigned through the admin interface.

•	Following the assignment specification, no user authentication is implemented. All Supabase Row Level Security policies allow anonymous read and write access. In a production system, authentication would be required for the Admin and Drivers pages.

•	BRN generation queries the highest existing BRN and increments it. Under high concurrent load this could theoretically produce duplicate BRNs. A production system would use a database sequence or unique constraint with retry logic.

•	The application has been tested in Chrome and Edge on Windows and macOS. The time input field renders in 12-hour or 24-hour format depending on the operating system locale, consistent with the Part 1 specification.

8. Reflection on AI-supported development process: 
Styling: The CSS design system in index.css and App.css was largely AI-generated. The initial output produced a functional but generic design. A follow-up prompt requesting a more professional colour scheme, consistent card components, and responsive grid layouts produced the current design. Minor manual adjustments were made to spacing and the confirmation box styling to match the Part 1 output format.

Map: Integrating Leaflet with React via react-leaflet required specific handling that the AI was aware of but needed prompting to apply correctly. The broken default icon URLs caused by Vite's asset handling were flagged by the AI in the first iteration and the CDN-based fix was included without requiring a separate debugging session. The suburb coordinate lookup table was manually reviewed and extended after the AI produced an initial list of ten suburbs, as several common Auckland suburbs relevant to the test data were missing.

Refactoring: Through my experience I found refactoring to be far easier to do using AI. AI was able to seperate all the files into sections and explain how exactly to setup each file in order to properly refactor the project